1
00:00:00,820 --> 00:00:08,860
Okay, now once you've gotten to the point where you understand that there are multiple carcinogens that can damage DNA,

2
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:20,480
then the next point that you have to understand is that there are particular regulatory systems that when damaged will certainly increase the risk for tumor growth and progression.

3
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:27,220
And so the next discussion that we want to have concerns these key regulatory systems.

4
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:41,640
So the three key regulatory systems that we're going to discuss are number one, the proto-oncogenes, number two, tumor suppressor genes, and number three, regulators of apoptosis.

5
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:45,860
These are three key systems that can be disrupted by carcinogenic agents.

6
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:48,620
And these are very high yield, very important.

7
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:52,360
There is a significant amount of biochemistry that goes into them.

8
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:56,960
So what we're going to do is we're actually going to discuss each of these in the subsequent.

9
00:00:57,220 --> 00:00:59,980
All right, what are proto-oncogenes?

10
00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:04,500
Proto-oncogenes are genes that are essential for cell growth and differentiation.

11
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:10,500
All right, and when mutated, so all our cells normally have proto-oncogenes,

12
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:13,600
and those proto-oncogenes are necessary for cell growth and differentiation.

13
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:19,480
However, when a proto-oncogene gets mutated, then it becomes something called an oncogene.

14
00:01:19,480 --> 00:01:24,760
And oncogenes, what they do is they lead to unregulated cell growth.

15
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:27,860
So oncogenes lead to unregulated cell growth.

16
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:30,860
Here are the key categories of oncogenes.

17
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:40,580
The categories of oncogenes include growth factors, growth factor receptors, signal transducers, and cell cycle regulators.

18
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:49,080
I think the best way to understand this notion of oncogenes is to go back and just remind ourselves of normal cellular growth.

19
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:55,480
And so I'm going to go ahead and generate a whiteboard here and just draw you a simple picture to remind you of something.

20
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:57,460
Let's pretend that this is a cell.

21
00:01:57,460 --> 00:02:08,920
Now, when this cell needs to divide, and there will be times when a cell needs to divide,

22
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:15,300
either because it's constantly turning over or because there is some sort of repair or regeneration, etc.

23
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:20,940
When this cell needs to divide, what happens is that growth factor comes in,

24
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:24,380
and growth factor binds to growth factor receptor.

25
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:30,980
And the binding of growth factor to growth factor receptor sends a signal,

26
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:35,520
and this signal is carried by something called a signal transducing molecule.

27
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:39,720
And so a signal transducer will carry the signal to the nucleus,

28
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:46,020
and within the nucleus, cell cycle regulators and nuclear regulators will get activated,

29
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:54,700
which will then cause the cell to cycle through G1S, G2M, and that's the normal cell cycle.

30
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:55,380
All right?

31
00:02:55,800 --> 00:03:02,600
So this is sort of the normal growth sequence, and each of these steps involves oncogenes.

32
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:07,420
So oncogenes include, or why don't we say, because we're discussing the normal,

33
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:09,600
each of these steps involves proto-oncogenes.

34
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:12,440
So one type of proto-oncogenes would be the growth factor,

35
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:15,740
another type of proto-oncogenes would be the growth factor receptor,

36
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:19,120
another type of proto-oncogenes would be the signal transducer,

37
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:23,540
and then another type of proto-oncogenes would be the cell cycle regulators.

38
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:26,620
So this is sort of the normal.

39
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:32,500
Now, in each of these cases, mutations in any one of these steps,

40
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:38,500
or any one of these subtypes of, or categories of proto-oncogenes,

41
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:43,940
can result in the formation of an oncogene and can then be associated with formation of a tumor.

42
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:49,020
And that takes us then to this table, which is present within your text.

43
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:55,020
And again, it's a fairly dense table, and so it might be easiest for you to follow along in your text here

44
00:03:55,020 --> 00:04:01,680
and then to write in the margin some of the notes or some of the points that I may make.

45
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:03,900
So let's take this step by step.

46
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:08,860
The first category of oncogenes are growth factors.

47
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:11,320
So that's way up here at the top of this table.

48
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:16,000
And one of the classic examples of this is astrocytoma,

49
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,660
which is the tumor of the astrocytes of the brain, which are a type of glial cell.

50
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:26,100
Now, I don't want to get into the details of what an astrocytoma is here,

51
00:04:26,180 --> 00:04:28,960
but I want to highlight to you that in this particular case,

52
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:34,140
what happens is that there is overexpression due to mutation of this growth factor,

53
00:04:34,380 --> 00:04:35,980
platelet-derived growth factor B,

54
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:42,580
and platelet-derived growth factor B gets overexpressed,

55
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:46,520
which then drives the formation of an astrocytoma.

56
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:49,920
So let me do this via illustration.

57
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:52,120
So let's pretend that this is a cell.

58
00:04:52,260 --> 00:04:54,080
This isn't an astrocyte.

59
00:04:54,160 --> 00:05:00,340
And let's pretend that this astrocyte simply has a platelet-derived growth factor receptor.

60
00:05:00,820 --> 00:05:00,960
Okay?

61
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:08,020
And normally, when platelet-derived growth factor binds the receptor,

62
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:09,780
the astrocyte then divides.

63
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:14,820
Now, what happens in this particular case is that you get a mutation

64
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:20,080
so that the astrocyte begins to overproduce platelet-derived growth factor.

65
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:23,940
And if the astrocyte begins to overproduce platelet-derived growth factor,

66
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:28,160
it can actually then sort of swing around and hit the same cell

67
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:31,900
through this signaling mechanism,

68
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:36,800
which then results in overgrowth of the cell,

69
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:40,980
i.e., the extra-platelet-derived growth factor keeps hitting the receptor.

70
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:43,800
The receptor then sends signal to the nucleus.

71
00:05:43,940 --> 00:05:47,340
The nucleus then recognizes that it needs to divide,

72
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:48,840
and so this cell grows.

73
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:52,340
And this becomes sort of a vicious cycle

74
00:05:52,340 --> 00:05:56,000
in which the cell keeps growing in an unregulated manner

75
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,780
and eventually produces an astrocytoma.

76
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:01,000
And so that's the classic example

77
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:07,460
where patients have a overexpression of platelet-derived growth factor,

78
00:06:07,460 --> 00:06:10,220
which then, in an autocrine loop,

79
00:06:10,540 --> 00:06:15,320
results in the overgrowth of astrocytes producing an astrocytoma.

80
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:20,260
So that's the first classic example of a oncogene mutation

81
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:23,620
or a mutated gene, i.e., oncogene,

82
00:06:23,980 --> 00:06:27,300
resulting in formation or being associated with formation of a tumor.

83
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:29,200
All right, so that's one example.

84
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:32,580
Another example would be growth factor receptors.

85
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,360
Another example, growth factor receptors.

86
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:41,560
So again, we can go back to our diagram,

87
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:44,400
and we can remind ourselves that if this is a cell,

88
00:06:44,820 --> 00:06:47,680
and the cell is expressing a growth factor receptor,

89
00:06:48,220 --> 00:06:49,640
all right, here it is,

90
00:06:49,660 --> 00:06:51,220
it's expressing a growth factor receptor.

91
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:54,200
When growth factor comes in,

92
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:57,680
all right, if normally what would happen

93
00:06:57,680 --> 00:06:59,180
and his growth factor would bind the receptor,

94
00:06:59,180 --> 00:07:00,300
a signal would be sent,

95
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:01,560
and the cell would then divide.

96
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:04,440
However, if you've got way too many receptors,

97
00:07:04,740 --> 00:07:05,720
let's say there's a mutation

98
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:07,580
and there's overexpression of this receptor,

99
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:11,260
then a simple stimulus of growth factor

100
00:07:11,260 --> 00:07:14,780
is going to result in a thousand times

101
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:18,180
or a hundred times signal to this cell to grow and divide,

102
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:21,860
and so this cell will begin to grow and divide out of control.

103
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:25,160
And that will eventually result in the formation of a tumor.

104
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:28,880
Now, what are some important growth factor receptors

105
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:30,000
that can become mutated,

106
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:31,860
and what tumors are they associated with?

107
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:34,960
Well, one of the very high-yield growth factor receptors

108
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:35,820
is HER2-NU.

109
00:07:36,480 --> 00:07:39,640
All right, HER2-NU is a growth factor receptor.

110
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:41,480
It's an epidermal growth factor receptor

111
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:44,380
that is amplified in a subset of breast cancers.

112
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:46,660
And why is this important to be familiar with?

113
00:07:46,740 --> 00:07:49,600
Because it ties back into pharmacology.

114
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:51,860
And you'll remember from pharmacology

115
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:56,060
that there is a designer antibody called trastuzumab.

116
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:58,640
The other name is Herceptin.

117
00:07:58,980 --> 00:08:01,560
But there is a designer antibody called trastuzumab,

118
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:04,380
which is designed against the HER2-NU receptor.

119
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:08,320
And so patients who have overexpression of HER2-NU,

120
00:08:08,980 --> 00:08:10,960
i.e. amplification of HER2-NU,

121
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:13,260
that results in breast cancer,

122
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:15,620
in a subset of breast cancer,

123
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:17,800
can be treated with trastuzumab.

124
00:08:18,220 --> 00:08:20,900
And there's a classic examination question

125
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:22,820
in which you could be asked,

126
00:08:23,320 --> 00:08:24,540
you know, a patient, I don't know,

127
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:25,600
we can make up a question

128
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:27,480
sort of along the same principles.

129
00:08:27,920 --> 00:08:31,840
Patient comes to you having read,

130
00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:33,860
a patient with a history of breast cancer

131
00:08:33,860 --> 00:08:37,500
comes to you having read an article in the newspaper

132
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:40,340
in which she sees that there is a new treatment

133
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:43,060
for breast cancer that is designed against,

134
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:45,000
that is a designer antibody

135
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:46,620
against a growth factor receptor.

136
00:08:47,080 --> 00:08:48,000
So we could ask them,

137
00:08:48,200 --> 00:08:50,060
which of the following tests

138
00:08:50,060 --> 00:08:51,760
would be important to perform

139
00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:53,800
in order to determine whether or not

140
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:55,120
she should be treated with the drug?

141
00:08:55,580 --> 00:08:56,960
A, estrogen receptor,

142
00:08:57,180 --> 00:08:58,960
B, progesterone receptor,

143
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:02,060
C, HER2-NU, et cetera, et cetera.

144
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:04,320
And obviously the answer would be HER2-NU.

145
00:09:04,320 --> 00:09:06,260
So it's important to be familiar with,

146
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:08,300
and I would basically say that

147
00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:10,280
in a subset of breast cancer,

148
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:13,640
there is amplification of the HER2-NU

149
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:14,700
growth factor receptor.

150
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:17,820
And so that's an important growth factor receptor

151
00:09:17,820 --> 00:09:19,020
to be familiar with.

152
00:09:19,340 --> 00:09:21,280
Another very important growth factor receptor

153
00:09:21,280 --> 00:09:22,800
to be familiar with is RET.

154
00:09:24,220 --> 00:09:26,900
And the key association with RET

155
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:29,300
is medullary carcinoma of the thyroid.

156
00:09:29,580 --> 00:09:32,400
In particular, medullary carcinoma of the thyroid

157
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:35,180
associated with MEN2A and 2B.

158
00:09:35,760 --> 00:09:37,640
Now, this requires a little bit of discussion.

159
00:09:37,780 --> 00:09:38,580
So what's MEN?

160
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:41,460
MEN is multiple endocrine neoplasia.

161
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:45,020
It is a syndrome in which patients develop tumors

162
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:46,660
in multiple endocrine organs.

163
00:09:47,040 --> 00:09:48,980
And it can be divided into MEN1,

164
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:51,740
MEN2A, and MEN2B.

165
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:54,420
And in MEN2A and 2B,

166
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:57,480
one of the common tumors

167
00:09:57,480 --> 00:09:59,460
between each of those 2A and 2B

168
00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:02,040
is medullary carcinoma of the thyroid.

169
00:10:02,700 --> 00:10:06,000
Now, this is a very high-yield association

170
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:10,680
because if you suspect MEN2A or 2B

171
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:11,480
in a patient,

172
00:10:11,860 --> 00:10:13,080
let's say a patient has

173
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:15,820
some of the other features of MEN2A or 2B,

174
00:10:15,940 --> 00:10:17,800
like pheochromocytoma

175
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:20,820
and, I don't know,

176
00:10:21,820 --> 00:10:23,020
mucosal neuromas,

177
00:10:23,020 --> 00:10:26,020
as would be seen in MEN2B,

178
00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:29,080
then those patients who,

179
00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:31,260
actually, those patients

180
00:10:31,260 --> 00:10:33,620
in whom you suspect MEN,

181
00:10:34,140 --> 00:10:35,900
you can do RET studies.

182
00:10:36,200 --> 00:10:37,820
And if the patient has a RET mutation,

183
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:39,020
then what you'll actually do

184
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:41,640
is prophylactically remove the thyroid,

185
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:44,360
i.e., the thyroid will be removed

186
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:46,420
before a tumor can actually form.

187
00:10:46,980 --> 00:10:49,500
And because medullary carcinoma of the thyroid

188
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:50,960
could potentially kill the patient.

189
00:10:50,960 --> 00:10:54,420
And so, testing for RET mutation

190
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:57,220
is a very important feature

191
00:10:57,220 --> 00:11:00,280
of how we might deal with patients

192
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:04,440
who have familial or MEN2A or 2B

193
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:07,200
associated medullary carcinoma of the thyroid.

194
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:09,620
So that actually is a very high-yield association.

195
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:11,520
And I'm going to give you much more detail

196
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:12,820
when we do the endocrine lectures

197
00:11:12,820 --> 00:11:14,540
and just, again, planting some seeds.

198
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:18,840
Another important growth factor receptor

199
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:21,600
is the KIT growth factor receptor.

200
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:25,100
And a mutation in the KIT growth factor receptor

201
00:11:25,100 --> 00:11:27,320
is associated with something called

202
00:11:27,320 --> 00:11:29,920
gastrointestinal stromal tumor.

203
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:32,940
And that's an important association

204
00:11:32,940 --> 00:11:33,900
to be familiar with.

205
00:11:34,460 --> 00:11:34,660
Okay.

206
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:37,240
The next group of oncogenes

207
00:11:37,240 --> 00:11:38,660
are signal transducers.

208
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:40,940
And the idea here

209
00:11:40,940 --> 00:11:42,980
is that after the growth factor receptor

210
00:11:42,980 --> 00:11:43,660
is bound,

211
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:45,140
it will send a signal

212
00:11:45,140 --> 00:11:46,420
using a transducer

213
00:11:46,420 --> 00:11:47,340
to the nucleus.

214
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:50,140
Now, imagine for a moment,

215
00:11:50,240 --> 00:11:51,440
let's go back to our whiteboard.

216
00:11:51,540 --> 00:11:52,980
Let's say that this is the cell

217
00:11:52,980 --> 00:11:55,720
and we've got this receptor here.

218
00:11:56,160 --> 00:11:57,460
If growth factor

219
00:11:57,460 --> 00:11:59,220
binds the growth factor receptor,

220
00:11:59,620 --> 00:12:01,380
then a message will be transduced

221
00:12:01,380 --> 00:12:02,880
and that message will go to the nucleus.

222
00:12:03,460 --> 00:12:04,120
Now, normally,

223
00:12:04,340 --> 00:12:06,280
for one growth factor receptor molecule,

224
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:07,360
you only want

225
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:09,280
one growth factor receptor

226
00:12:09,280 --> 00:12:10,880
signal

227
00:12:13,660 --> 00:12:15,260
that the messenger gets mutated

228
00:12:15,260 --> 00:12:16,640
so that the messenger

229
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:18,720
keeps sending signal

230
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:20,000
to the nucleus to grow.

231
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:22,260
If there's massive signal

232
00:12:22,260 --> 00:12:23,980
heading to the nucleus to grow,

233
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:25,460
the nucleus will obviously

234
00:12:25,460 --> 00:12:26,820
cause the cell to divide

235
00:12:26,820 --> 00:12:28,640
and multiple divisions

236
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:29,880
will eventually result

237
00:12:29,880 --> 00:12:31,680
in the formation of a cancer.

238
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:33,980
And so that's the idea

239
00:12:33,980 --> 00:12:38,200
in this particular subtype

240
00:12:38,200 --> 00:12:40,680
of oncogenes.

241
00:12:40,740 --> 00:12:41,200
And that is

242
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:42,600
that if there are mutations

243
00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:43,700
in signal transducers,

244
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:45,420
that'll result in the formation

245
00:12:45,420 --> 00:12:46,500
of a cancer.

246
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:48,480
And so what are some classic ones

247
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:49,420
to be familiar with?

248
00:12:49,820 --> 00:12:50,740
Well, the first

249
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:52,280
and probably the most important

250
00:12:52,280 --> 00:12:53,260
is the RAS

251
00:12:53,260 --> 00:12:58,520
signal transducing protein.

252
00:12:59,860 --> 00:13:00,800
Why is this important?

253
00:13:01,220 --> 00:13:03,320
Because it is commonly mutated

254
00:13:03,320 --> 00:13:04,840
in a large number of cancers.

255
00:13:04,980 --> 00:13:05,240
In fact,

256
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:08,020
I think up to 70 to 80%

257
00:13:08,020 --> 00:13:09,080
of human tumors

258
00:13:09,080 --> 00:13:12,400
actually have a RAS mutation

259
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:14,060
associated with that tumor.

260
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:15,000
So it's seen

261
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:16,440
in multiple carcinomas,

262
00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:18,560
it's seen in melanoma,

263
00:13:18,700 --> 00:13:19,680
and in lymphoma.

264
00:13:20,020 --> 00:13:22,120
So a broad range

265
00:13:22,120 --> 00:13:23,540
of human cancers

266
00:13:23,540 --> 00:13:25,620
actually have a mutation

267
00:13:25,620 --> 00:13:28,940
within the RAS signal transducer.

268
00:13:29,440 --> 00:13:31,740
This then requires us

269
00:13:31,740 --> 00:13:33,020
to go into a little discussion

270
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:33,820
of biochemistry.

271
00:13:34,380 --> 00:13:34,920
So I'm going to take

272
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:35,780
a step back here.

273
00:13:35,780 --> 00:13:37,680
This discussion is written

274
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:38,200
in your book,

275
00:13:38,320 --> 00:13:38,940
but I'm going to go ahead

276
00:13:38,940 --> 00:13:39,860
and present it here

277
00:13:39,860 --> 00:13:42,660
because it's the perfect place

278
00:13:42,660 --> 00:13:43,200
in which to have

279
00:13:43,200 --> 00:13:43,820
this discussion.

280
00:13:44,200 --> 00:13:45,440
So I'm going to simply

281
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:46,520
go back and produce

282
00:13:46,520 --> 00:13:47,260
my whiteboard

283
00:13:47,260 --> 00:13:48,680
and just let you know

284
00:13:48,680 --> 00:13:49,880
a little bit about RAS.

285
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:51,940
So what's the idea

286
00:13:51,940 --> 00:13:52,520
with RAS?

287
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:53,500
Let's pretend

288
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:54,680
that this is a cell,

289
00:13:55,140 --> 00:13:56,880
and the cell clearly

290
00:13:56,880 --> 00:13:57,880
has a receptor,

291
00:13:58,660 --> 00:14:00,000
and growth factor

292
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:01,240
would come in,

293
00:14:01,860 --> 00:14:02,660
and growth factor

294
00:14:02,660 --> 00:14:03,680
would bind that receptor.

295
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:05,280
Now, when growth factor

296
00:14:05,280 --> 00:14:06,200
binds the receptor,

297
00:14:06,420 --> 00:14:07,320
one of the molecules

298
00:14:07,320 --> 00:14:08,500
that can be associated

299
00:14:08,500 --> 00:14:09,340
with a receptor

300
00:14:09,340 --> 00:14:11,600
is actually called RAS.

301
00:14:12,160 --> 00:14:13,360
R-A-S, okay?

302
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:15,200
So that's RAS.

303
00:14:15,620 --> 00:14:17,440
Now, when RAS

304
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:18,260
is associated

305
00:14:18,260 --> 00:14:19,320
with a receptor,

306
00:14:19,660 --> 00:14:20,760
it usually sits

307
00:14:20,760 --> 00:14:21,880
in its off state,

308
00:14:21,980 --> 00:14:22,940
which is basically

309
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:24,540
RAS associated

310
00:14:24,540 --> 00:14:26,000
with something called GDP.

311
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:27,000
All right?

312
00:14:27,440 --> 00:14:28,280
Now, the moment

313
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:29,660
that growth factor receptor

314
00:14:29,660 --> 00:14:30,780
binds the receptor,

315
00:14:31,400 --> 00:14:33,380
the GDP gets kicked off,

316
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:34,500
and instead,

317
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:36,020
RAS gets associated

318
00:14:36,020 --> 00:14:37,580
with GTP.

319
00:14:38,180 --> 00:14:38,980
And when RAS

320
00:14:38,980 --> 00:14:39,680
gets associated

321
00:14:39,680 --> 00:14:40,480
with GTP,

322
00:14:41,120 --> 00:14:42,960
the RAS-GTP complex

323
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:44,640
then can send message

324
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:45,860
down to the nucleus.

325
00:14:47,100 --> 00:14:48,780
And that's the key notion

326
00:14:48,780 --> 00:14:49,140
of how,

327
00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:50,360
that's the key way

328
00:14:50,360 --> 00:14:51,380
by which RAS works.

329
00:14:51,960 --> 00:14:53,920
Now, because RAS has the,

330
00:14:54,080 --> 00:14:54,920
RAS-GTP,

331
00:14:55,620 --> 00:14:56,840
when associated together,

332
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:57,680
has the ability

333
00:14:57,680 --> 00:14:58,640
to carry the message

334
00:14:58,640 --> 00:14:59,600
down to the nucleus,

335
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:01,100
you don't want

336
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:02,180
too much message

337
00:15:02,180 --> 00:15:02,980
sent to the nucleus,

338
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:04,020
you simply want

339
00:15:04,020 --> 00:15:05,120
a message sent

340
00:15:05,120 --> 00:15:05,740
to the nucleus.

341
00:15:06,280 --> 00:15:06,580
And so,

342
00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:08,720
RAS also has the ability

343
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:09,960
to cut away

344
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:11,100
one of the phosphates

345
00:15:11,100 --> 00:15:12,540
so that the GTP

346
00:15:12,540 --> 00:15:13,840
becomes GDP,

347
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:15,700
allowing RAS

348
00:15:15,700 --> 00:15:16,860
to become shut off.

349
00:15:18,220 --> 00:15:20,160
And RAS has the ability

350
00:15:20,160 --> 00:15:20,700
to cut away

351
00:15:20,700 --> 00:15:21,260
that phosphate.

352
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:22,980
This is so important

353
00:15:22,980 --> 00:15:24,140
that there is

354
00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:25,100
an additional molecule

355
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:26,260
associated with RAS,

356
00:15:26,260 --> 00:15:27,340
which is called GAP,

357
00:15:27,840 --> 00:15:29,460
which is GTPase

358
00:15:29,460 --> 00:15:32,380
associated protein.

359
00:15:32,660 --> 00:15:33,880
And the GTPase

360
00:15:33,880 --> 00:15:34,840
associated protein

361
00:15:34,840 --> 00:15:35,900
helps RAS

362
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:37,540
to cleave that phosphate

363
00:15:37,540 --> 00:15:38,640
to shut off

364
00:15:38,640 --> 00:15:40,040
the on form

365
00:15:40,040 --> 00:15:41,020
or the active form

366
00:15:41,020 --> 00:15:41,500
of RAS.

367
00:15:42,060 --> 00:15:43,840
Now, in RAS mutations,

368
00:15:44,140 --> 00:15:45,380
one of the common mutations

369
00:15:45,380 --> 00:15:46,160
is the mutation

370
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:47,920
in this associated protein.

371
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:49,200
So that if this

372
00:15:49,200 --> 00:15:50,820
GTPase associated protein

373
00:15:50,820 --> 00:15:51,380
is missing,

374
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:53,200
RAS is going to have

375
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:54,580
difficulty shutting off

376
00:15:54,580 --> 00:15:55,660
GTP,

377
00:15:56,260 --> 00:15:58,880
or converting GTP

378
00:15:58,880 --> 00:15:59,580
to GDP

379
00:15:59,580 --> 00:16:00,440
and therefore

380
00:16:00,440 --> 00:16:01,140
will not be able

381
00:16:01,140 --> 00:16:01,740
to shut off.

382
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:03,220
And so when RAS

383
00:16:03,220 --> 00:16:04,540
remains associated

384
00:16:04,540 --> 00:16:05,280
with GTP,

385
00:16:05,920 --> 00:16:06,660
this will send

386
00:16:06,660 --> 00:16:07,700
excess signal

387
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:09,860
to the nucleus

388
00:16:09,860 --> 00:16:11,120
causing the cell

389
00:16:11,120 --> 00:16:12,180
to overgrow.

390
00:16:13,020 --> 00:16:14,780
And that's the key

391
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:16,340
issue concerning RAS.

392
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:17,200
So the biochemistry

393
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:18,220
of RAS is important

394
00:16:18,220 --> 00:16:20,140
and the fact

395
00:16:20,140 --> 00:16:20,940
that it's associated

396
00:16:20,940 --> 00:16:22,100
with so many human

397
00:16:22,100 --> 00:16:23,160
cancers

398
00:16:23,160 --> 00:16:24,780
is important

399
00:16:24,780 --> 00:16:25,840
and makes this molecule

400
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:26,400
high yield.

401
00:16:26,500 --> 00:16:27,060
And that's why

402
00:16:27,060 --> 00:16:28,460
I spent a little time

403
00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:29,300
going over it.

404
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:30,840
Okay.

405
00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:32,720
So that's RAS.

406
00:16:32,760 --> 00:16:33,100
And let's see

407
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:33,980
what we say here

408
00:16:33,980 --> 00:16:34,420
in the book.

409
00:16:34,840 --> 00:16:35,940
The RAS G family

410
00:16:35,940 --> 00:16:39,280
is a GTP binding protein.

411
00:16:39,940 --> 00:16:40,140
Okay.

412
00:16:40,520 --> 00:16:41,520
And a mutation

413
00:16:41,520 --> 00:16:42,820
within RAS,

414
00:16:42,900 --> 00:16:43,500
in particular

415
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:45,020
within the GTPase

416
00:16:45,020 --> 00:16:47,040
activating protein

417
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:49,220
present within RAS,

418
00:16:49,220 --> 00:16:50,520
can result in

419
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:52,660
multiple human cancers.

420
00:16:54,280 --> 00:16:54,660
All right.

421
00:16:54,900 --> 00:16:55,580
Next important

422
00:16:55,580 --> 00:16:56,440
signal transducer

423
00:16:56,440 --> 00:16:57,520
is the ABL

424
00:16:57,520 --> 00:16:59,020
signal transducer.

425
00:16:59,200 --> 00:17:00,140
This is a tyrosine

426
00:17:00,140 --> 00:17:00,700
kinase.

427
00:17:01,180 --> 00:17:01,660
When there's

428
00:17:01,660 --> 00:17:03,440
a 922 translocation,

429
00:17:03,880 --> 00:17:05,620
there is overactivation

430
00:17:05,620 --> 00:17:06,660
of this tyrosine

431
00:17:06,660 --> 00:17:07,120
kinase.

432
00:17:07,540 --> 00:17:08,860
And this overactivation

433
00:17:08,860 --> 00:17:09,880
classically results

434
00:17:09,880 --> 00:17:10,560
in CML.

435
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:11,900
So this is another

436
00:17:11,900 --> 00:17:13,080
example of over

437
00:17:13,080 --> 00:17:14,080
expression of a

438
00:17:14,080 --> 00:17:15,040
signal transducer.

439
00:17:15,420 --> 00:17:15,720
Now,

440
00:17:15,880 --> 00:17:17,020
it's important to note

441
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:18,720
that the 922 translocation

442
00:17:18,720 --> 00:17:20,320
defines CML.

443
00:17:20,880 --> 00:17:22,200
It defines CML.

444
00:17:22,660 --> 00:17:23,260
But also,

445
00:17:23,400 --> 00:17:24,140
I would be aware

446
00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:24,660
of the fact

447
00:17:24,660 --> 00:17:25,540
that the 922

448
00:17:25,540 --> 00:17:26,260
translocation

449
00:17:26,260 --> 00:17:27,060
is also seen

450
00:17:27,060 --> 00:17:29,240
in some types

451
00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:29,900
of ALL.

452
00:17:30,460 --> 00:17:31,160
And in particular,

453
00:17:31,380 --> 00:17:33,040
in adult ALL.

454
00:17:33,220 --> 00:17:33,620
Now, remember

455
00:17:33,620 --> 00:17:34,220
that ALL

456
00:17:34,220 --> 00:17:36,220
is basically

457
00:17:36,220 --> 00:17:37,240
a disease of children.

458
00:17:37,720 --> 00:17:38,080
However,

459
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:38,920
in some,

460
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:40,480
in adults

461
00:17:40,480 --> 00:17:41,280
that form ALL,

462
00:17:41,760 --> 00:17:42,240
there is an

463
00:17:42,240 --> 00:17:42,740
increased,

464
00:17:42,980 --> 00:17:45,460
there is an

465
00:17:45,460 --> 00:17:46,600
increased propensity

466
00:17:46,600 --> 00:17:47,960
to have this

467
00:17:47,960 --> 00:17:48,740
translocation,

468
00:17:48,840 --> 00:17:50,240
a 922 translocation.

469
00:17:50,380 --> 00:17:51,080
It's also called

470
00:17:51,080 --> 00:17:54,000
PH-positive ALL,

471
00:17:54,360 --> 00:17:54,680
i.e.

472
00:17:54,780 --> 00:17:55,260
Philadelphia

473
00:17:55,260 --> 00:17:56,740
chromosome-positive ALL,

474
00:17:56,880 --> 00:17:57,800
because the 922

475
00:17:57,800 --> 00:17:58,600
translocation

476
00:17:58,600 --> 00:17:59,700
is also called

477
00:17:59,700 --> 00:18:00,140
the Philadelphia

478
00:18:00,140 --> 00:18:00,680
chromosome.

479
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:01,840
And recall that

480
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:02,860
that ALL actually

481
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:03,540
has a relatively

482
00:18:03,540 --> 00:18:04,640
poor prognosis,

483
00:18:04,820 --> 00:18:05,200
i.e.

484
00:18:05,600 --> 00:18:06,840
ALL with a 922

485
00:18:06,840 --> 00:18:07,800
translocation

486
00:18:07,800 --> 00:18:08,960
has a relatively

487
00:18:08,960 --> 00:18:10,040
poor prognosis.

488
00:18:10,760 --> 00:18:11,380
So that's another

489
00:18:11,380 --> 00:18:13,240
type of oncogene

490
00:18:13,240 --> 00:18:14,780
that can be mutated,

491
00:18:14,880 --> 00:18:15,140
i.e.

492
00:18:15,140 --> 00:18:16,200
signal transducers.

493
00:18:17,080 --> 00:18:18,220
The next type

494
00:18:18,220 --> 00:18:18,760
of oncogene

495
00:18:18,760 --> 00:18:20,360
that can be mutated

496
00:18:20,360 --> 00:18:21,300
are the nuclear

497
00:18:21,300 --> 00:18:21,860
regulators.

498
00:18:22,140 --> 00:18:22,920
And the nuclear

499
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:23,380
regulators,

500
00:18:23,540 --> 00:18:24,020
they're basically

501
00:18:24,020 --> 00:18:24,560
a group of

502
00:18:24,560 --> 00:18:25,580
transcription factors,

503
00:18:26,020 --> 00:18:26,400
and these

504
00:18:26,400 --> 00:18:27,340
transcription factors

505
00:18:27,340 --> 00:18:28,360
go to the nucleus

506
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:29,700
and cause

507
00:18:29,700 --> 00:18:31,140
upregulation of genes

508
00:18:31,140 --> 00:18:32,520
necessary for growth.

509
00:18:32,920 --> 00:18:33,040
Okay?

510
00:18:33,380 --> 00:18:33,880
And so those

511
00:18:33,880 --> 00:18:34,760
are nuclear regulators.

512
00:18:34,920 --> 00:18:35,640
And the most important

513
00:18:35,640 --> 00:18:36,460
nuclear regulator

514
00:18:36,460 --> 00:18:37,740
is MYC,

515
00:18:38,180 --> 00:18:38,980
M-Y-C.

516
00:18:39,240 --> 00:18:39,880
That's the most

517
00:18:39,880 --> 00:18:40,640
important nuclear

518
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:41,060
regulator.

519
00:18:41,060 --> 00:18:42,040
And what I would

520
00:18:42,040 --> 00:18:43,460
remind yourself of

521
00:18:43,460 --> 00:18:44,780
here is that

522
00:18:44,780 --> 00:18:46,260
the three key

523
00:18:46,260 --> 00:18:46,920
MYCs to be

524
00:18:46,920 --> 00:18:50,760
familiar with

525
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:52,020
are C-MYC,

526
00:18:52,560 --> 00:18:53,320
which is associated

527
00:18:53,320 --> 00:18:53,920
with Burkitt's

528
00:18:53,920 --> 00:18:54,360
lymphoma,

529
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:55,600
N-MYC,

530
00:18:55,800 --> 00:18:56,280
and the N

531
00:18:56,280 --> 00:18:57,080
helps you to remember

532
00:18:57,080 --> 00:18:58,240
neuroblastoma,

533
00:18:58,760 --> 00:18:59,640
and L-MYC.

534
00:19:00,020 --> 00:19:00,860
And L-MYC is

535
00:19:00,860 --> 00:19:01,880
associated with

536
00:19:01,880 --> 00:19:03,320
small cell lung

537
00:19:03,320 --> 00:19:03,960
carcinoma.

538
00:19:04,080 --> 00:19:05,060
So the L will help

539
00:19:05,060 --> 00:19:05,520
you to remember

540
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:05,940
lung.

541
00:19:05,940 --> 00:19:07,580
So each of these

542
00:19:07,580 --> 00:19:08,360
are transcription

543
00:19:08,360 --> 00:19:08,840
factors.

544
00:19:09,040 --> 00:19:09,720
MYC is a

545
00:19:09,720 --> 00:19:10,600
transcription factor.

546
00:19:11,080 --> 00:19:12,120
And upregulation

547
00:19:12,120 --> 00:19:13,440
or overexpression

548
00:19:13,440 --> 00:19:16,600
of the MYC gene

549
00:19:16,600 --> 00:19:17,660
will result in

550
00:19:17,660 --> 00:19:18,960
overproduction

551
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:22,200
of growth-promoting

552
00:19:22,200 --> 00:19:22,840
proteins,

553
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:24,040
and that will

554
00:19:24,040 --> 00:19:24,660
result in

555
00:19:24,660 --> 00:19:25,360
overgrowth,

556
00:19:25,480 --> 00:19:26,320
increasing the risk

557
00:19:26,320 --> 00:19:26,860
for development

558
00:19:26,860 --> 00:19:28,560
of a particular

559
00:19:28,560 --> 00:19:29,400
type of cancer.

560
00:19:30,020 --> 00:19:30,660
Now one thing

561
00:19:30,660 --> 00:19:31,140
that I would like

562
00:19:31,140 --> 00:19:32,060
to highlight here

563
00:19:32,060 --> 00:19:34,840
is the fact

564
00:19:34,840 --> 00:19:36,240
that when you

565
00:19:36,240 --> 00:19:37,040
have a C-MYC

566
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:38,400
translocation,

567
00:19:39,800 --> 00:19:41,800
that that will

568
00:19:41,800 --> 00:19:42,520
result in

569
00:19:42,520 --> 00:19:43,580
Burkitt lymphoma.

570
00:19:44,660 --> 00:19:44,960
All right?

571
00:19:45,380 --> 00:19:46,920
And it's important

572
00:19:46,920 --> 00:19:47,720
to note here

573
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:49,160
that the C-MYC

574
00:19:49,160 --> 00:19:49,640
translocation,

575
00:19:49,780 --> 00:19:50,980
the mechanism

576
00:19:50,980 --> 00:19:52,020
by which C-MYC

577
00:19:52,020 --> 00:19:53,020
is overexpressed

578
00:19:53,020 --> 00:19:53,820
in this particular,

579
